// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// PAPER TRACK v1 DRAFT (ENGLISH, <=1500 words) — export to markdown
// for the Kaggle Writeup at submit time (strip leading "// ").
// Track: ARC-AGI-3 (ARC-AGI-2 as laboratory). P-1 pending: public
// notebook attachment + repo publication before final submit.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// TITLE: Learning to See: A Verify-Then-Route Loop for ARC
// SUBTITLE: Small rule sets, honest detectors, and milestones that
// transfer from static puzzles to interactive games.
//
// --- INTRO (~150w) ---
// ARC-AGI-2 gives input-output pairs; ARC-AGI-3 gives a grid, actions,
// and silence until a level completes. Most approaches attack one track.
// Ours treats both as the same problem: RECOGNIZE the dynamic, then act.
// A recognizer classifies what a puzzle (or game state) IS ABOUT from
// observations; verified strategies execute; a reporter ranks what to
// hunt next. Every claim in this paper is measured, including the zeros:
// we publish refutations with the same care as solves.
//
// --- METHOD (~450w) ---
// CLASSIFY-THEN-ROUTE. For static puzzles, detectors propose a dynamic
// (recolor, tiling, symmetry, movement, fill...) and each detector must
// REPRODUCE every training pair (leave-one-out) before it may touch the
// test — detectors prove generalization, not recall. A nearest-neighbor
// memorizer that passed naive checks was caught exactly this way and
// excluded. Routing tries the single predicted strategy first (precision
// @1), falling back to the full chain, so routing can only reorder, never
// regress: zero regressions across the whole series.
// For interactive games the same loop runs online: a fixed probe suite
// maps each action to its effect (cells, regions, color deltas); grids
// embed as R^23 vectors, so runs become trajectories; MILESTONES are
// displacements above an adaptive threshold (5x run median), which solves
// the sparse-reward problem without hand-tuning per game. Visited-state
// hashing (tabu) and dead-click memory prevent re-trying the void.
// A second loop hunts autonomously: cluster ranking orders targets,
// a composer tries depth-2 strategy pairs, a dead-ends registry (3,258
// entries) guarantees no failure is ever repeated. It found nothing —
// also published, also data: real rules here are mechanisms, not pairs.
// Human demonstrations plug in at the same interface (a 2/6-level play
// dissected into probe/transform/check phases became a game branch).
//
// --- RESULTS (~350w) ---
// ARC-AGI-2 (1000 training puzzles): blind fixed-order baseline 1.3%,
// routed 3.9% (precision@1 3.8%), toolbox oracle 4.4%, 20 dynamics, 19
// solving, efficiency 8.3 tries of 30, full run ~1 minute, bit-identical
// across re-runs from re-pulled data. Every gain shipped with a LOO
// proof and zero regressions; four refutations archived with evidence.
// ARC-AGI-3 (25 local games): first level via button repetition (5-click
// threshold, position-gated), second game via a human-derived bulk+check
// cycle; a 5-game probe library maps each game's action effects, and an
// adaptive milestone detector separates cursor noise from bulk transforms
// without per-game tuning. Scorecards published per run.
//
// --- THEORY (~250w) ---
// Why it works: verification-before-routing bounds overfitting by
// construction (a rule cannot fire where it cannot reproduce). Milestones
// are shaped reward with a derivation, not a hack: displacement bands in
// embedding space separate micro/medium/large events across games.
// Failure analysis is first-class: lockouts (an early check permanently
// voids a level), viability complements (dead-ends map exactly where the
// system must not go), and family fragmentation (extract/draw/split each
// split into sub-rules on measurement) are reported, not hidden. This is
// second-order cybernetics with teeth: the observer (reporter, milestones,
// dead-ends) is part of the loop it improves.
//
// --- UNIVERSALITY + PROGRESS (~150w) ---
// One taxonomy drives both tracks (move/paint/transform families map to
// cursor/interact/bulk action families); the category catalog maintains
// itself (new solving dynamics self-register with precision, examples,
// and origin). All code, reports, and negative results open (link);
// anyone can reproduce the numbers or extend the toolbox — each addition
// is one detector plus one measurement.
//
// --- CLOSE (~50w) ---
// Small verified loops beat large unverified ones. Our numbers are
// modest; our method is auditable end to end, and every zero taught us
// where the next point lives.
//
// Word budget: ~1400. Assets needed: cover image + public notebook link.

